  "analysis": "We need to maximize the total treasure collected by choosing any connected subset of mountains (a connected subgraph of the given tree) such that the sum of construction costs of the edges used to connect that subset is within the budget W. Since moving to a different mountain requires constructing the roads along the path, the set of edges used forms a connected subtree. The problem reduces to: given a tree with node values p[i] and edge costs c[i], find the maximum sum of node values over any connected subset whose connecting edges total cost is <= W. A dynamic programming on trees (tree knapsack) approach fits: root the tree, and for each node u compute dp[u][t] = maximum treasure sum of a connected subtree that includes u and is contained within the subtree rooted at u, with total edge cost t. When merging a child v with edge cost w = c(u,v), we can either ignore the child's subtree or attach a connected subtree from v; if we attach, we pay w plus some t’ from v. We combine via a knapsack-like merge. The final answer is the maximum value across all nodes u and all t <= W of dp[u][t], since any connected subset has a unique topmost node with respect to the chosen root. N ≤ 100 and W ≤ 100000, so we implement the DP with pruning by tracking feasible costs per subtree to keep it practical. The code below implements this approach and tests all sample cases by running the compiled program with sample inputs.",
