- For pB[v] >= t: distance = |t - (-d[v])| where d[v] = pA[v] - pB[v], so it's the distance from t to s1[v] = -d[v].
- For pB[v] < t: distance = |t - (n - d[v])|, so it's the distance from t to s2[v] = n - d[v].

Thus, for each t, the answer is min( distance from t to any s1[v] among values with pB[v] >= t, distance from t to any s2[v] among values with pB[v] < t ).

We can compute this efficiently by sweeping t from 0 to n-1, maintaining two multisets:
- S1 contains s1[v] for all v with pB[v] >= t.
- S2 contains s2[v] for all v with pB[v] < t.

When we increase t by 1, values with pB[v] == t drop from S1 and move to S2. For each t, we query the nearest value to t in S1 and S2 (using lower_bound in a multiset to check the closest predecessor and successor) to get A and B, then answer[t] = min(A, B). This yields O(n log n) time and O(n) memory.

The implementation below follows this plan in C++17, reading n, a, b; computing pA, pB, d, s1, s2; performing the sweep; and printing the answers for t = 0..n-1 (which correspond to the 1st through n-th shifts as required).

